From smj@cirr.com  Wed Dec 26 19:55:05 2001
From: Stephen M Jones <smj@cirr.com>
To: port-alpha@netbsd.org
Date: Wed, 26 Dec 2001 19:55:05 +0000
Subject: AS1200 memory question.

The AS1200 can have up to 4 gigs of RAM.  The type of modules it
uses are PC66 ECC SDRAM registered unbuffered .. there are 16 slots,
so that means it could handle 256MB modules.

I've been told that I could use PC100 ECC SDRAM registered unbuffered ..
That the slower bus could handle modules that normally handle higher
bus speeds .. is that true?

From smj@cirr.com  Wed Dec 26 20:39:01 2001
From: Stephen M Jones <smj@cirr.com>
To: port-alpha@netbsd.org
Date: Wed, 26 Dec 2001 20:39:01 +0000
Subject: AS1200 Memory question.

The AS1200 can support 4gigs of PC66 ECC SDRAM registered 
unbuffered.  The maximum size of each module being 
256mb (256mb * 16 slots = 4096mb).

I was told that PC100 ECC SDRAM registered unbuffered 
will work as well.  Infact, faster bus speed RAM will
work as long as it will meet the other requirements.  

I'm wondering if others are doing this?  When you look
at the prices of RAM today where the only difference
between the module is PC66 vs PC100 (other than the
price being double for the PC66!) you'd have to ask
this question too.

Thanks!

From schaecsn@gmx.de  Wed Dec 26 19:31:13 2001
From: schaecsn <schaecsn@gmx.de>
To: smj@cirr.com
Date: Wed, 26 Dec 2001 19:31:13 +0000
Subject: Re: AS1200 Memory question.

Hi Stephen,

I just tried it for PCs. I do use 100Mhz modules in a 66Mhz system. I
was told that you can even 133Mhz modules.

I know of no reason why an Alpha should be different.

- Stefan

